# **A MEASUREMENT OF A TREE IS NOT MERGED, IT IS RE-TAKEN.**
#
# `src/adcp/types/verdicts.tsv` and `verify.json` are what `./verify` writes: one row per
# graded pair, with each check's verdict.  They are a function of the tree they graded, so
# two branches that both regenerate produce two different measurements of two different
# trees, and git has no business reconciling them line by line -- the result would be a
# table describing neither.
#
# Measured cost of not saying so: the file blocked a rebase in THREE lanes in one session
# ("CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in src/adcp/types/verdicts.tsv"), and the correct
# resolution each time was `git checkout --ours` followed by `./verify`, because either
# side is equally wrong until the artifact is re-taken against the merged tree.
#
# `merge=ours` makes git take the current branch's copy and stop asking.  It is NOT a
# claim that the copy is right: it is a claim that the question is the wrong one, and the
# answer is `./verify`.  `tests_emit/test_the_verdicts_are_green.py` asserts the artifact
# names THIS checkout's head, so a copy carried across a merge reddens rather than passing
# -- which is what keeps this from being a silent exemption.
src/adcp/types/verdicts.tsv merge=ours linguist-generated=true
src/adcp/types/verify.json  merge=ours linguist-generated=true

# The emitted tree itself, for the same reason one layer along: `codegen` writes it from the
# bundle, so a conflict there is resolved by regenerating and never by choosing lines.  Kept
# diffable (no `-diff`) because a reader comparing two emissions is exactly the review this
# project does.
src/adcp/types/_schemas/** merge=ours
